title A law learns a second home
prompt The baton, and the card's fourth agent-doable item -- the Glow gate law ratchet standing at its floor of 14, wanting a design lap rather than a sweep.
think The scan's own header did the hardest part of this lap before I arrived. It read the fourteen unlinked desks ONE AT A TIME and sorted them into three kinds, then named the exact condition under which the floor could fall again -- a struct field count, an enum variant count, or a const whose value is an expression. That is a header written for the next hand rather than for its own author.
think Crux first, among the three shapes. The grammar's KIND is the crux: once a law's middle field can select which reader answers, each home is a small plug. So I changed the grammar once and attached two readers rather than special-casing either shape.
think The suffix rides on the NAME rather than arriving as a fourth field, and the reason is subtraction. A fourth field would make all eleven standing law lines carry a `const` token they do not need, which rewrites correct declarations to buy nothing. A Zig identifier can never hold a dot, so `Line.fields` can never be mistaken for a constant called that.
think The struct reader already stood, built for REDS %500 when four guards proved a field count with presence greps. The enum reader did not, so that is the one new instrument, claimed on the board and pushed before the build.
obs THE ZERO RULE IS WHAT MAKES A REFUSAL HONEST. Both count readers print `0` beside a non-zero exit when they cannot find what was asked for, so a law naming an absent struct would resolve to zero and be reported as `body_disagree` -- a WRONG diagnosis where `law_unresolved` is the true one. I rejected zero at the call site, and the mutation proving it flips three legs from law_unresolved to body_disagree. Nothing is lost: the population passes over a literal zero already, so no desk in it can hold a lawful law of zero.
obs A CLAUSE NOTHING CAN BREAK IS A CLAUSE DOING NO WORK. My first draft of the enum reader carried two comment clauses, one for `///` and one for `//`. Removing the `///` one changed no reading at all, because every `///` line begins `//`. I dropped it, said so in the header, and added a plain-comment leg so the surviving clause is proven from its own side -- removing it now fails four legs.
obs THE FOUR MANTRA DESKS WERE ALREADY HELD, by a different scan under mantra_a1_equality. So `unlinked` counted desks THIS scan did not hold, never desks nothing held. Two guards over one claim is the honest cost, and neither subsumes the other: the elder pair also asserts field ORDER, which a count cannot see. The scan's header says so now rather than letting a reader assume the desks were unguarded.
obs THE WITNESS COULD NOT HEAR ITS OWN CONTROL'S LEG COUNT. `control_verdict=ok` says only that the control reached its last line, so the five legs I wrote could have read wrongly under a green witness -- the unheard-guard shape one room over. The witness asserts `legs=51` now, and names each new home's leg, so a leg written tomorrow is heard the day it lands.
obs AURORA'S SIX LIVING STAGES ARE A FOURTH KIND OF HOME, and I found that by reading rather than assuming. They are not a const, a struct, or an enum: they are the six files of aurora/src/ that are stages, out of eight. A module roster count. Named in the header for the lap that builds it.
obs THE TWO AURORA LENGTH DESKS STAY UNREACHABLE ON PURPOSE. tally/kumara.rye declares them as Ed25519 expressions, and the module's own `assert(seed_length == 32)` is a SECOND statement of the law rather than the declaration -- pointing a law line at it would compare two copies and call that agreement.
